          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Iron deficiency anemia sits in the blood chapter. Which chapter would an underlying bleeding ulcer be coded from?",
          "answer": "A bleeding peptic ulcer would be coded from the digestive system chapter, not the blood chapter. The digestive chapter (Chapter 11, K00–K95) covers the stomach and duodenum, and it has its own categories for bleeding presentations such as hematemesis, melena and unspecified gastrointestinal hemorrhage (source: ICD-10-CM Chapter 11: diseases of the digestive system).\n\nThe iron deficiency anemia itself stays in the blood chapter. A related rule from the blood chapter guide is that chronic blood loss causing iron deficiency is coded from whichever chapter owns the bleeding site (source: ICD-10-CM Chapter 3: blood, blood-forming organs and the immune mechanism). So the bleeding site decides where the cause is coded.\n\nThe search results did not include the specific ulcer or hemorrhage codes, so I cannot give the exact code numbers. Coding decisions depend on how the clinical record is documented, so a medical coder or the treating clinician should confirm them.\n\nIf you or someone you know is having vomiting of blood, black or tarry stools, dizziness, fainting or severe stomach pain, seek emergency care right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
